WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.689
let's go to the phones at (617) 830-4750 and
see what's on people's minds.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:13.050
Let's start with our color from the seven
eight six area code caller from seven eight

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.940
six uh, who, who are you?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.560
Where are you calling from?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.300
Is this me?

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.300
That is you.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.300
All right.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.300
My name is Christian.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.090
I'm a student from Florida.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.100
Hey Christian, what's going on?

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.369
Okay, so

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:32.950
I just wanted to ask you what, you could have
kind of had this idea recently that I think

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:39.560
the United States should move maybe more direct
democracy.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.330
So I just want to see, what do you think would
be the best mechanism to achieve that?

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.800
Because I had, I just going to throw an idea
out there.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.120
You can tell me what you think about it.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.120
Yeah.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.820
Imagine someone, for example, like Bernie
Sanders becomes president and he passes an

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:01.850
executive order that allows a law or maybe
a constitutional amendment to be achieved

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.620
by a direct national popular vote.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.620
Yeah.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.600
How, how do you think that's possible?

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.600
Do you think that's something that like someone
should strive for?

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.790
Listen, there are a number of different ways
that our democracy could become more direct.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:24.150
One of them, as you're suggesting, is replacing
the electoral college with a national popular

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.150
vote.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.150
That's a good idea.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.890
The other thing you have to consider is that
when corporations and rich people have disproportionate

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:35.640
amounts of power and their desires are far
more likely to become law or policy than those

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.640
of someone in the middle class or the lower
middle class or whatever, that is a form of

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:45.430
making democracy more indirect by which it
is not an issue of the number of people that

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.940
want something, but more an issue of the economic
power that they hold.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.729
So yes, I think there are a number of ways
that we can make our democracy more direct.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:54.380
I've been talking about some of them for awhile.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.780
Uh, and I think it's really great that you're
thinking about at Christian, but there's something

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.390
very strange happening on, on your phone that
sounds like crinkling papers.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:06.549
So I'm going to let you go cause it's a little
bit painful to hear, but I appreciate the

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:08.660
call in a very, very important topic.

